@meta
id: 23-05
title: Complete a path from the disk
chapter: 23
type: lesson
skills: complete-file
par: 25
minutes: 4
vscode: the path IntelliSense, if the extension is installed
open: seed.ts

@brief
The seed script loads three CSV fixtures. The first path is complete;
the other two stop after a couple of letters. The files exist in
`fixtures/` next to this script, and their names are long enough that
you do not want to type them, or remember them.

Complete the two paths from the disk. Do not open the directory to
look; the point is that vim will look for you.

@keys
| `Ctrl-X Ctrl-F` (insert mode) | complete a filename from the filesystem |
| `Ctrl-N` / `Ctrl-P` | move through the candidates |

@hints
Get the cursor onto the closing quote of the second path — `4G$F'` —
and `i` to insert just before it.
--
`Ctrl-X Ctrl-F` reads the partial path to the left of the cursor
(`fixtures/or`) and completes it against the files that actually
exist.
--
`Esc`, then `j$F'i` and the same chord for the refunds line.

@solution
    4G$F' i<Ctrl-X><Ctrl-F><Esc>
    j$F' i<Ctrl-X><Ctrl-F><Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty-one keystrokes, and no typos in filenames, ever.

`Ctrl-X Ctrl-F` takes the filename characters before the cursor and
completes them the way the shell would. Relative paths are relative to
vim's working directory. A prefix that matches several files opens the
usual menu; `Ctrl-N` moves through it. `fixtures/` on its own, with
nothing after the slash, lists everything in the directory, which is a
fast way to browse without leaving insert mode.

The quote before the path is not a filename character, so vim knows
where the path starts. The same goes for `(`, `,` and a space.

Two more members of the family you should know exist. `Ctrl-X Ctrl-]`
completes from a tags file. `Ctrl-X Ctrl-O` is omni completion — the
one that knows what type a variable is and what methods it has. It
needs something to ask: a language server, which is chapter 30.

@start seed.ts
import { loadCsv } from './load-csv';

const customers = loadCsv('fixtures/customers-2024-q3.csv');
const orders = loadCsv('fixtures/or');
const refunds = loadCsv('fixtures/re');

export const seed = { customers, orders, refunds };

@expected seed.ts
import { loadCsv } from './load-csv';

const customers = loadCsv('fixtures/customers-2024-q3.csv');
const orders = loadCsv('fixtures/orders-2024-q3-placed.csv');
const refunds = loadCsv('fixtures/refunds-2024-q3-approved.csv');

export const seed = { customers, orders, refunds };

@start fixtures/customers-2024-q3.csv
id,email,name
c_1001,ana@example.com,Ana Costa
c_1002,ben@example.com,Ben Okafor

@start fixtures/orders-2024-q3-placed.csv
id,customerId,status,total
o_2001,c_1001,PENDING,120.00
o_2002,c_1002,SHIPPED,48.50

@start fixtures/refunds-2024-q3-approved.csv
id,orderId,amount
r_3001,o_2002,48.50
